<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Aedui</h1><p class="st-meta">300 BCE – 51 BCE (approx.) · capital Bibracte</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Leading people of central Gaul and Rome's oldest Gallic ally, whose quarrels with their rivals gave Caesar his pretext to invade Gaul.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Aedui held the land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saone">Saône</a>, in modern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Burgundy</a>, with their great oppidum at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bibracte">Bibracte</a> on Mont Beuvray. By the 2nd century BCE they were hailed by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commune-of-rome">Roman Senate</a> as brothers and kinsmen of the Roman people, and they contested leadership of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/arverni">Arverni</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sequani">Sequani</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Government was oligarchic: a council of nobles and an annual chief magistrate, the vergobret, who could not leave the territory during office. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bibracte">Bibracte</a> was a large town of craftsmen, enamellers and traders importing Italian wine. The druid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diviciacus-aedui">Diviciacus</a>, who had pleaded the Aeduan cause before the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commune-of-rome">Roman Senate</a>, led the pro-Roman party, while his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dumnorix">Dumnorix</a> led the anti-Roman party.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Pressed by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sequani">Sequani</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariovistus">Ariovistus</a>, the Aedui called on Caesar in 58 BCE, but in 52 BCE they joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a>, who was chosen supreme leader at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bibracte">Bibracte</a>. Pardoned after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-alesia">Alesia</a>, they moved to the new city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/autun">Augustodunum</a> (Autun), and in 48 CE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> admitted Aeduan nobles to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commune-of-rome">Roman Senate</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/aedui.jpg" alt="Aedui" width="320" height="173"><figcaption>Image: Eugène Hucher (1814-1889) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dumnorix_monnaie_d%27argent_15mm.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>300 BCE – 51 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bibracte</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Diviciacus, Dumnorix</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gaulish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Haedui</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-176&amp;focus=aedui" aria-label="Aedui on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4775 530 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Aedui lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5095 786L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5095 786h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5095 786h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bibracte. <a href="/atlas?year=-176&amp;focus=aedui">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

